{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond of Antioch was born in 1140 and died in 1187. He was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\nHe became count of Tripoli at the age of 12 after his father's death.\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was involved in a series of conflicts throughout his reign. One of the most significant conflicts was the Battle of Artah in 1164. *Battle of Artah*\n\nAt the Battle of Artah, Raymond III Count of Tripoli led the combined forces of the County of Tripoli and the Principality of Antioch against the forces of Nur ad-Din, the Zengid emir of Aleppo and Mosul. The battle was a crushing defeat for the Crusader forces, and Raymond III Count of Tripoli was captured and imprisoned for 16 years.\n\nAfter his release, Raymond III Count of Tripoli resumed his rule in Tripoli and continued to face conflicts with the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties. Another significant conflict was the Siege of Homs in 1164. *Siege of Homs*\n\nDuring the Siege of Homs, Raymond III Count of Tripoli attempted to break the siege of the city of Homs by the Zengid forces. However, the attempt was unsuccessful, and Raymond III Count of Tripoli was forced to retreat.\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli also had a complex relationship with the Byzantine Empire. In 1180, he negotiated a marriage between his daughter Melisende and the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus. However, the marriage never took place due to Manuel I's death in 1180.\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states in the Levant. He was a skilled military leader and politician who faced numerous challenges throughout his reign. His rule was marked by conflicts with the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties, as well as by his attempts to maintain alliances with the Byzantine Empire.\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin, a disastrous defeat for the Crusader forces at the hands of the Ayyubid Sultan Saladin. His death marked the end of the County of Tripoli as an independent Crusader state. *Battle of Hattin*\n\nOverall, Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a significant figure in the history of the Crusader states in the Levant. His reign was marked by conflicts, alliances, and negotiations, reflecting the complex and challenging political landscape of the time.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001627074379939586}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.3794739544391632}, {"token": "of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -8.940656698541716e-06}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "also", "logprob": -0.515670657157898}, {"token": "known", "logprob": -0.0033385988790541887}, {"token": "as", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -7.867782187531702e-06}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -1.053788423538208}, {"token": "Ant", "logprob": -0.03495628386735916}, {"token": "io", "logprob": -0.0036044646985828876}, {"token": "ch", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.006574784871190786}, {"token": "born", "logprob": -1.1671749353408813}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -0.03818347677588463}, {"token": "", "logprob": -2.5152843591058627e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "4", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": "and", "logprob": -0.052579574286937714}, {"token": "died", "logprob": -0.0007035640883259475}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -0.0004306104383431375}, {"token": "", "logprob": -0.0034627497661858797}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"toke